  {
    "key": "LNRVJRKF",
    "version": 27757,
    "itemType": "journalArticle",
    "title": "Comparative Analysis of Metaheuristic Algorithms for Optimizing the Placement of Electric Vehicle Charging Infrastructure on Highways with Cartographic Visualization of the Obtained Solutions",
    "creators": [
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "A N",
        "lastName": "Rozhkov"
      },
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "M Elkholy",
        "lastName": "Akhmed"
      },
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "M G",
        "lastName": "Astashev"
      },
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "I A",
        "lastName": "Cherdintsev"
      },
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "A V",
        "lastName": "Badalyan"
      }
    ],
    "abstractNote": "This paper presents a comparative analysis of four metaheuristic algorithms for the placement of electric vehicle charging infrastructure. The analysis is based on the calculation of installation locations and the selection of charging station capacity on the M–11 Neva highway. A genetic algorithm, particle swarm method, differential evolution, and simulated annealing (selection) are considered. All methods solve the same problem on the same demand model, use the same computational resources, a single set of scenarios, and the same conditions. Five different operating scenarios were studied for these algorithms, differing in power grid operating conditions, highway traffic, etc., with each scenario analyzed under different initial conditions. The study combines the results of over 38,000 objective function calculations. It is shown that, according to a complex indicator that combines the quality of the objective function, project implementation cost, stability, and computation time, the particle swarm method proved to be the best, but it is also the most expensive and the slowest. The nonparametric Friedman’s test of integrated utility confirmed that the differences between the algorithms are statistically significant. A route mapping demonstrates that the identified advantage method achieves its benefits primarily by reducing infrastructure density and overall cost, rather than by maximizing the service level of charging stations.",
