  {
    "key": "CFPX55EN",
    "version": 24533,
    "itemType": "journalArticle",
    "title": "Statistically robust genetic algorithm planning of EV fast-charging corridors under grid and storage constraints",
    "creators": [
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "Ahmed M.",
        "lastName": "Elkholy"
      },
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "Dmitry I.",
        "lastName": "Panfilov"
      },
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "Alexander N.",
        "lastName": "Rozhkov"
      },
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "Artush V.",
        "lastName": "Badalyan"
      },
      {
        "creatorType": "author",
        "firstName": "Ivan A.",
        "lastName": "Cherdintsev"
      }
    ],
    "abstractNote": "Growing electric vehicle (EV) adoption demands well-planned fast-charging networks along intercity corridors, yet most planning studies rely on single optimization runs whose results cannot be statistically reproduced. This paper presents a genetic algorithm (GA) framework that jointly optimizes charger placement, capacity, grid interaction, battery storage dispatch, and renewable integration for long-distance highway corridors, and—crucially—validates the outcomes through a 50-run statistical protocol. The framework is applied to the 669 km Moscow–St. Petersburg M-11 motorway under ﬁve stress-test scenarios spanning diﬀerent EV adoption rates, grid capacities, charger sizes, and storage conﬁgurations. Across scenarios the optimizer achieves mean service rates of 74–81% (74.09–80.98%) at mean infrastructure costs of $8.33–10.63M. One-way ANOVA conﬁrms that scenario choice signiﬁcantly aﬀects performance (𝐹 (4, 45) = 7.67, 𝑝 < 0.001, 𝜂2 = 0.41), while the mean coeﬃcient of variation of 4.11% demonstrates that the GA delivers reproducible solutions. Among the tested conﬁgurations, concentrating higher-capacity chargers at fewer sites (Capacity Push) yields the best mean service rate (81.0%) at moderate cost ($8.67M), whereas distribution-grid limits—not demand—prove to be the binding constraint on optimization quality. These ﬁndings provide a statistically grounded case-study basis for corridor planning while identifying the need for future power-ﬂow-coupled grid models and more advanced BESS control.",
